  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+4x+4.$",
  "worked_solution": "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{\u2033}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =4x+4 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${f}^{\u2033}(x)=0$ has no real solutions, and so there are no points of inflection.",
